<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Serbian Despotate</h1><p class="st-meta">1402 CE – 1459 CE · capital Smederevo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Last medieval Serbian state, which held out against the Ottomans for half a century from its fortresses on the Danube and its rich silver mines.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kosovo">battle of Kosovo</a> in 1389, Lazar's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-lazarevic">Stefan Lazarević</a> ruled <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/moravian-serbia">Moravian Serbia</a> as an Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>. Following the Ottoman defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a> in 1402, the Byzantine emperor granted him the title of despot, and he made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> his capital with Hungarian backing. In 1427 the despotate passed to his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durad-brankovic">Đurađ Branković</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The despotate drew great wealth from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novo-brdo">Novo Brdo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srebrenica">Srebrenica</a>, worked partly by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> miners and traded through <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-ragusa">Ragusa</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-lazarevic">Stefan Lazarević</a> was a writer and patron, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/resava-school">Resava school</a> of scribes refined Serbian Church Slavonic. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durad-brankovic">Đurađ</a> built the vast fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smederevo">Smederevo</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> between 1428 and 1430.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> overran <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/serbia">Serbia</a> in 1439, but the Peace of Szeged restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durad-brankovic">Đurađ</a> in 1444. After his death in 1456 and that of his son Lazar in 1458, internal quarrels left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smederevo">Smederevo</a> exposed, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> took it in 1459, ending Serbian independence until the 19th century.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/serbian-despotate.jpg" alt="Serbian Despotate" width="320" height="287"><figcaption>Image: Ivan25 (translated by Vorziblix) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Serbian_Despotate_(1422)-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1402 CE – 1459 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Smederevo</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Stefan Lazarević, Đurađ Branković, Lazar Branković</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Serbian Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Serbian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a>, <a href="/countries/kosovo">Kosovo</a>, <a href="/countries/montenegro">Montenegro</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Despotate of Serbia, Despotovina</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1430&amp;focus=serbian-despotate" aria-label="Serbian Despotate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5168 594 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Serbian Despotate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5488 850L5513 889M5488 850L5446 783M5488 850L5709 970M5488 850L5512 917M5488 850L5459 926"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5513 889h0M5446 783h0M5709 970h0M5512 917h0M5459 926h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5488 850h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5488 850h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1430&amp;focus=serbian-despotate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
